  "verbalization": "Betamethasone, a corticosteroid, is implicated in the treatment of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) through a shared pathway involving 17Alpha-Hydroxy Steroid and Therapeutic Glucocorticoid. The evidence suggests that Betamethasone, through its treatment of allergic rhinitis and asthma, ultimately leads to the treatment of SLE via glucocorticoids. This connection is reinforced by the lowest common ancestors of the entities involved, which highlight the role of corticosteroids in the therapeutic cascade. The overlapping pathways, such as the treatment of allergic rhinitis by prednisolone or triamcinolone, further support the notion that Betamethasone's effects on SLE are mediated through glucocorticoid mechanisms. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of corticosteroids in the treatment of autoimmune diseases like SLE, with Betamethasone serving as a key node in this therapeutic network.",
